**Amendment (Nesting-aware operational artifacts)** · `amend:observe:artifact-batch-sync` Refines (`amend:observe:compact-content`) and supersedes the per-object synchronization clause of (`amend:observe:accounting-representation-closure`). Small values below the {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-inline-byte-threshold') }}-byte inline threshold return immediately when their structural nesting fits; depth and exact JSON size are computed in one bottom-up traversal after the inline early return, without reserializing every large ancestor. A deep subtree is chunked only when its remaining inline nesting reaches the limit, rather than at every absolute depth boundary. Fresh object bytes are written before atomic replacement and verified; files and directories take no synchronization at all, because neither these artifacts nor their referring observation stream promises durability; these operational artifacts make no crash-durability promise and are outside binding computation under (`inv:observe:noninterference`). The deep_payload_delivery and compaction_branching test-site witnesses report elapsed compaction seconds and object counts, while reconstruction and parseability remain the predicates. Final-status transport limitations remain as stated in (`amend:observe:delivery-cause-and-exit`).


**Amendment (Reader-bound nesting and measured runtime allowance)** · `amend:observe:runtime-calibration` Refines (`amend:observe:artifact-batch-sync`). Inline payload nesting is {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-inline-nesting-level-count') }} levels, retaining {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-reader-headroom-level-count') }} levels of headroom below the default serde_json recursion limit of {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-serde-json-default-recursion-limit') }} for record envelopes and artifact references. The compatibility readers are the default serde_json reader used by Rust delivery/reconstruction witnesses and Python json used by the runtime comparisons and optional observation-log splitter; the export bridge carries the complete serialized JSON as text without reparsing it. Every referenced object and envelope remains separately parseable, and content reconstruction remains exact. The {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-inline-byte-threshold') }}-byte inline threshold is unchanged. The four-package trace changes from {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-prior-artifact-reference-count') }} artifact references and {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-prior-artifact-object-count') }} unique objects to {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-current-artifact-reference-count') }} references and {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-current-artifact-object-count') }} objects per run; its inline stream correspondingly grows from about {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-prior-inline-stream-bytes') }} bytes to {{ numval('num:numbers:observe-current-inline-stream-bytes') }} bytes. The ordinary gate remains at default emission. The observation-runtime suite and gate step now share an {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.registry', name='observation-runtime').number_forms.budget_ms | display }}-millisecond warning allowance through (`expt:devperf:observation-runtime-suite`) and (`expt:devperf:observation-runtime-step`): {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.registry', name='observation-runtime').number_forms.warning_marg