**Amendment (Lossless operation counters)** · `amend:observe:atomic-operation-counters` (`inv:observe:noninterference`) and (`conv:observe:metrics`) require declared counters, including started and completed operations, to update atomically and remain present in snapshots without taking the lossy totals mutex. Exact runtime comparisons read these lossless series and remain unconditional; finite integer overflow is separately disclosed as lost counter updates, never silently wrapped. Counter increments perform no SDK call; SDK observable-counter callbacks read those same atomic totals when the reader collects, keeping SDK synchronization off binding callers. The production summarize path collects and exports before its JSON snapshot; the production-order witness checks both started and completed totals there and on a second collection. No exported-delta bookkeeping or snapshot side effect is required. Gauges and histograms retain nonblocking try-lock updates and report contention loss independently. The held-lock contention witness establishes update and snapshot independence from those mutexes; this does not turn a concurrent multi-series snapshot into a transactional cut.

**Amendment (Host-intranet export)** · `amend:observe:host-intranet` The exporter transport of (`amend:observe:export-credentials`) is a host-intranet link under (`rec:intranet:decision`) and the collector link's explicit case (`inv:intranet:explicit-case`). The first realization uses (`case:intranet:host-secured`), relying on the operator for the secure path to the collector; the implementation-guarded transport remains (`open:intranet:guarded-transport`). Collector reachability, credentials and retained transport errors remain secret-owned, while public telemetry bodies remain fully observable. Public SDK placeholders, refusal of overriding dependency configuration and the requirement to admit the actual service transport remain unchanged; selecting a case does not settle (`inv:observe:secret-boundary`).

**Amendment (Signed exporter credential)** · `amend:observe:jwt-credentials` The service credential under (`amend:observe:export-credentials`) is a signed JWT under (`dec:intranet:signed-tokens`), verified by the collector under (`rule:intranet:token-verification`). The existing ExportTransport boundary passes a protected credential separately from the SDK's public OTLP request, so the JWT must remain outside ordinary SDK header and endpoint state. The current local transport fixture compares a literal secret credential; it has no JWT parser or ES256K verifier and establishes neither collector compatibility nor a production exception. No actual collector implementation or version is declared, so its ES256K capability remains unestablished. Before service export is enabled, the telemetry integration implementer must name the collector and witness verification and secret-preserving transport, or record that link's evidenced exception if the receiver cannot verify ES256K or the integration cannot carry a JWT safely; no exception may weaken (`inv:observe:secret-boundary`). Candidate encoding inputs for later admission are base64 0.22.1 with no default features for slice-based base64url encoding, serde_json 1.0.154 using its writer interface, sha2 0.11.1 with zeroize for the admitted new SHA-256 integration; the existing 0.10.9 line remains for current consumers until their separately reviewed migration, and secp256k1-zkp 0.11.2 with its reviewed secp256k1 0.29.1 and sys bindings under (`open:crypto:dependency-admission`). Secret-owned input/output and parser failures need their own review; naming codecs does not admit a generic JSON value, temporary public String or token-bearing error. No additional JWT signing backend is selected: the encoding layer must call the base library. The token recipe, codec integration, collector verification and real transport remain held under (`open:design:secret-integrations`); the local fixture remains only controlled evidence of the boundary it exercises.

**Amendment (Artifact digest implementing line)** · `amend:observe:sha-two-line` (`amend:identity:sha-two-line`) completes the first-party move of the artifact SHA-256 implementation to the already admitted sha2 0.11.1 line; the deferred first-party migration named in (`amend:observe:jwt-credentials`) is complete. The content-address recipe and reconstruction checks remain unchanged, and no JWT integration or token recipe is admitted by this dependency move.


**Amendment (Storage instrument registration)** · `amend:observe:storage-instruments` Under (`model:design:observation-map`), HOST's existing storage stem additionally registers exactly eight counters (encoded and decoded bytes, queries, successor attempts, commits, rollbacks, retries and refusals), two gauges (active transactions and queue depth), and three histograms (package bytes and query and transaction elapsed seconds). No other owner gains these names; undeclared names still refuse registration. Query counts cover the closed statement executions; byte counts cover canonical package production and validated readback. Rollbacks count abandoned or explicitly rolled-back transaction handles, not a conclusion about an uncertain commit; CommitUnknown remains explicit. Queue depth measures pending succession locks. Completion and timing stay nonbinding and never enter canonical storage or replay equality.

**Amendment (Graph accounting owner)** · `amend:observe:graph-owner` Under (`sig:design:graph`) and (`conv:observe:names`), GRAPH has its own accounting dispatch and graph contract metric stem. The graph_owner_accounting witness exercises one GRAPH-labelled operation at default and trace with an injected clock, checking its own invocation count and elapsed sample and zero invocations charged to other owners. This registers the accounting prerequisite; it does not establish formation, dependency partition, geometry or their domain work counters. Those operations and their runtime evidence remain obligations of (`gate:design:bite-3`).

**Amendment (Labelled secret exits)** · `amend:observe:secret-exits` Repository amendment: (`rule:observe:privacy`) keeps private values in secret carriers and records all public content. The necessary suspension of that representation guarantee follows (`rule:observe:secret-exceptions`), rather than being an unrecorded convention. A borrowed reference, a mutable reference, an initializer's ordinary temporary, or a copy into an ordinary address, scalar, cryptographic state or protocol buffer is an exposure even when it is never logged. This does not permit filtering public observations.

**Rule (Secret exposure exceptions)** · `rule:observe:secret-exceptions` Secret exposure is denied by default, like unsafe code. Each explicit exit has its own Caveat label, the closest declared genre for a local limitation of the representation guarantee, and the smallest enclosing function has an unconditional `#[allow(clippy::disallowed_methods)]` immediately preceded by a complete Justified sentence. The exception names the material, its destination, lifetime and the reason the consumer cannot accept a carrier. Each exit has a separate consumer-specific reason; a function-local Justified sentence may summarize its lint permission but cannot replace those reasons. The census refuses reused reasons across distinct labels in both the source and register, normalizing whitespace, case and final punctuation. It also joins each source Caveat reason to its register reason by label and refuses drift. Normalization collapses whitespace, folds case and removes a final period; a source-local ROOT citation is rendered as the same parenthesized, backtick-delimited local label used in the record, while other citation text is retained. This comparison does not erase a changed destination, lifetime or cited label. The register remains authored so its projection is reviewed, but cannot silently disagree with the source exception. Semantic differences and downstream lifetimes still require review. Container-preserving alternatives take precedence. Borrowed buffer accessors are themselves exposure boundaries; the host buffer and configuration accessors and the authentication-payload accessor are also denied methods, so every call has its own labelled consumer exception. Distinct accessor names let the source census refuse an unlabelled call or method value without confusing ordinary byte APIs. Configuration field and text borrows, its transient ordinary port and authentication-payload borrows retain unique denied accessor names, so forwarding does not conceal the next consumer. Buffer length is already public bookkeeping and is read without exposing its bytes. Keeping the byte-oriented consumers makes their existing protocol and cryptographic contracts explicit; silently sharing a broad accessor permission would hide new destinations. The classified-public frame observation has its own exception after classification; no private observation is authorized. Review every ordinary copy downstream, including transient configuration scalars and cryptographic library state, against that exception; a new destination or lifetime needs a new reviewed exception. Clippy controls exercise method-call syntax, trait-qualified calls, method values and initializer calls, both denied and item-locally permitted; the source census accounts individual labels and the smallest item's allow and justification. Neither check proves semantic data flow, absence of copied secrets or macro-expanded consumer safety; those remain reviewer-confirmed obligations with negative leakage probes under (`proposal:observe:enforcement`). The census prints sites and distinct labels beside the suppressions census and retains the source locations as an artifact. Secret serialization is not admitted.

**Convention (Fixture secret boundaries)** · `conv:observe:fixture-secret-exits` Tests retain the suppression ban except for `clippy::disallowed_methods` on an explicitly documented fixture-only secret boundary function. Each exit still needs its own Caveat label and justification. Such a boundary may acquire, compute or inspect only secrets created by that test or its isolated harness; it accepts no deployment secret. It may be the test function itself or a local helper when the test's local carrier type and acquisition or drop assertions require that scope. A generic unrestricted exposure helper would conceal fixture provenance rather than establish it. The gate refuses other test suppressions, module or implementation-wide allows, inherited permission from a parent function, missing labels and repeated labels. Fixture provenance is reviewed, not inferred from the function name. This narrowly amends the test-suppression clauses of (`proposal:observe:enforcement`) and (`dec:lint:policy`), without weakening the claims under test.


**Register (Reviewed secret exits)** · `reg:observe:secret-exits` Each exception is minted here and cited at its individual source exit. Function-local lint permission does not extend to a nested function or to an uncited exit. The source census joins each citation, path, smallest function and method to this register; removed or additional exits refuse until the register is reviewed with them. All listed exits are retained; the consumer APIs require ordinary borrows or values. Fixture entries authorize only isolated test material under (`conv:observe:fixture-secret-exits`).

